 ## Overview
-Level-synchronized bisection ray casting is a robust algorithm for rendering the zero-level-set of an implicit function $f: \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}$ by processing all active rays in lockstep (batch) using interval arithmetic to guarantee no surface features are missed [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of missing fine surface details or grazing intersections inherent in standard discrete ray marching by bounding the function value over continuous segments and iteratively bisecting intervals until convergence [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its parent [Kalra & Barr Implicit Surface Intersection](1278-kalra-barr-implicit-surface-intersection.md) by explicitly leveraging interval arithmetic for inclusion testing and batch synchronization for performance, and from its sibling [Bisection Ray Tracing with Interval Inclusion](1314-bisection-ray-tracing-with-interval-inclusion.md) by its specific implementation of level-synchronized processing and Hermite-based root estimation [S1, unofficial][S2].
